WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.215
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.215 --> 00:00:08.555
It is September 13, 1833,

00:00:08.555 --> 00:00:14.390
in the Northeastern Indian
port city of Kolkata.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:18.905
It is 90 degrees outside and
the air is humid.

00:00:18.905 --> 00:00:23.942
You're in a crowd anxiously waiting,
unsure if you're about to

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:28.900
see a historical feat of human
ingenuity or an elaborate prank.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:35.152
Then slowly and steadily off the horizon,
a brig named Tuscany approaches.

00:00:35.152 --> 00:00:41.713
In it, 100 tons of ice, cut in blocks and
stacked neatly against one another,

00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:47.150
having just finished a four month voyage,
all the way from Boston.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:55.361
What you just witnessed was the journey
along one of the first international

00:00:55.361 --> 00:01:01.385
temperature-controlled supply chain,
also known as the cold chain.

00:01:01.385 --> 00:01:04.887
The man behind this cold
chain was Frederic Tudor,

00:01:04.887 --> 00:01:07.420
deservedly nicknamed The Ice King.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.442
Tudor started this icy empire by
harvesting natural ice in the dead of

00:01:13.442 --> 00:01:18.419
New England winter, then delivering
it first across the United States and

00:01:18.419 --> 00:01:21.110
eventually to Great Britain and India.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:27.503
Whilst this journey was long and
its historical significance monumental,

00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:32.757
Tudor's cold chain from a logistical
perspective was extraordinarily simple.

00:01:32.757 --> 00:01:36.873
For a long time, the only commodity
he transported was ice and

00:01:36.873 --> 00:01:41.240
ice kept itself cool as long as
there was sufficient insulation.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.710
So Tudor invested heavily in these
ice houses along the ports and

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.260
his business took off.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:55.150
Now in the two centuries that followed,
the cold chain had come a long way.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:02:00.200
Today, the cold chain is more complex and
more global than ever before.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.988
Just to give you a sense of its scale and
complexity, nowadays for a piece of

00:02:05.988 --> 00:02:10.780
imported fruit to get to you and me,
it will roughly go through 20 steps.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.630
Here's a chart just to
show you what that's like.

00:02:17.156 --> 00:02:22.199
In the last three years working as a food
investor, I have seen this process

00:02:22.199 --> 00:02:27.724
in play day in and day out, in lettuce
fields, in sew plants, and in refrigerated

00:02:27.724 --> 00:02:32.792
trucks delivering imported cheese to
pizzerias all across New York City.

00:02:32.792 --> 00:02:37.290
I marveled at this well oiled
machine that connects growers,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.971
manufacturers, distributors,
retailers, and consumers.

00:02:41.971 --> 00:02:48.323
The cold chain enables us to introduce
diversity and abundance into our diet.

00:02:48.323 --> 00:02:52.080
It removes seasonality and
distances from food.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.563
It gives us choices so
that food is not just about survival, but

00:02:57.563 --> 00:02:59.174
part of our identity.

00:02:59.174 --> 00:03:04.212
That is, until this all stopped working.

00:03:04.212 --> 00:03:08.088
Many of these images may
look familiar to you.

00:03:08.088 --> 00:03:11.270
Bagel shops without cream cheese.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.263
Starbucks without Frappuccino.

00:03:13.263 --> 00:03:16.050
KFC without chicken tenders.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.905
For me, a broken cold chain meant
spending hours on the phone with

00:03:21.905 --> 00:03:26.750
our logistics providers,
telling them how unacceptable it was for

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.771
them to leave frozen ready meals in
a hot truck for 24 hours too long.

00:03:31.771 --> 00:03:35.574
Our cold chain is susceptible
to disruptions, and

00:03:35.574 --> 00:03:40.094
we've had an awful lot of
disruptions in the last two years.

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:44.025
Not only that,
the cold chain is also fossil fueled and

00:03:44.025 --> 00:03:46.512
incredibly energy-intensive.

00:03:46.512 --> 00:03:50.817
According to the UN,
the food cold chain accounts for

00:03:50.817 --> 00:03:54.269
about 1.2 gigatons of co2 emissions.

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.793
That is about 1% of total
global annual emissions.

00:03:58.793 --> 00:04:03.411
So in between worrying about
the revenue loss from customers

00:04:03.411 --> 00:04:08.028
receiving spoiled food, and
the costs associated with that

00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:13.026
extra pack of dry ice I now need to
budget for it in the deliveries.

00:04:13.026 --> 00:04:18.775
I couldn't help but wonder,
how can we make this better?

00:04:18.775 --> 00:04:24.854
One solution came to mind, and
it's almost too obvious and too logical.

00:04:24.854 --> 00:04:27.677
Why not simplify the supply chain?

00:04:27.677 --> 00:04:30.782
Do what the industry calls nearshoring.

00:04:30.782 --> 00:04:35.886
Take out a few nodes here and there,
shorten the distance traveled,

00:04:35.886 --> 00:04:38.528
source locally, eat farm to fork.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:44.263
It's simple, sustainable,
and more resilient, right?

00:04:44.263 --> 00:04:46.165
Not so fast.

00:04:46.165 --> 00:04:51.388
To illustrate just how complicated
the cold chain is and how hard it is to

00:04:51.388 --> 00:04:57.586
disentangle even a tiny portion of it, let
me take you on a journey of one commodity.

00:04:57.586 --> 00:05:01.056
Walk into any Western European
supermarket and

00:05:01.056 --> 00:05:06.290
you will see these beautiful
uniform fresh French green beans.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:11.967
These beans were in the fields just 48
hours ago in the rural villages outside

00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:18.520
Bobo-Dioulasso in Burkina Faso, among many
other former European colonies in Africa.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.108
These beans were handpicked by local
manual labor when it's hot and

00:05:24.108 --> 00:05:27.745
sunny outside, and
then immediately chilled to about 40

00:05:27.745 --> 00:05:30.958
degrees Fahrenheit to
ensure high export quality.

00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:34.660
They're then loaded onto
refrigerated trucks to be packed at

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.090
a temperature-controlled facility.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:44.815
Then, lastly, loaded onto a jet
flying directly to Paris.

00:05:44.815 --> 00:05:50.112
Now what is missing from this picture
of seamless modern miracle is the fact

00:05:50.112 --> 00:05:55.007
that many of these villages don't
have paved roads or electricity.

00:05:55.007 --> 00:06:00.418
These farmers rely on charcoal to chill
the beans instead of refrigerators,

00:06:00.418 --> 00:06:05.596
before sending the beans to an air
conditioned facility to be processed.

00:06:05.596 --> 00:06:10.476
Many of these farmers also have come
to rely on this high value export crop

00:06:10.476 --> 00:06:12.643
as their sole source of income.

00:06:12.643 --> 00:06:18.379
And they import shelf stable food for
their own consumption.

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:21.152
Now if we decide that we
want to in source the beans,

00:06:21.152 --> 00:06:25.621
these farmers would lose their livelihood
and the local economy would collapse.

00:06:25.621 --> 00:06:28.458
But perhaps most ironically,

00:06:28.458 --> 00:06:33.595
those who grow the beans cannot
even afford to eat them.

00:06:33.595 --> 00:06:38.324
The lack of cold storage meant
that most of these beans would rot

00:06:38.324 --> 00:06:42.350
before the locals could get
a chance to consume them.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:47.951
You see,
eating locally requires refrigeration,

00:06:47.951 --> 00:06:52.795
and refrigeration is simply too expensive.

00:06:52.795 --> 00:06:58.642
In fact, scientists estimate
that as much as 40% of the fresh

00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:05.266
food produced in Africa is lost due
to inadequate cold chains annually.

00:07:05.266 --> 00:07:09.714
Globally, the carbon
footprint of food loss and

00:07:09.714 --> 00:07:16.233
food waste is four times the carbon
footprint of the cold chain itself.

00:07:16.233 --> 00:07:21.905
So counter intuitively for
us to solve for a less carbon-intensive

00:07:21.905 --> 00:07:27.985
food ecosystem means adding cold
chain capacity and adding it quickly.

00:07:27.985 --> 00:07:33.988
And since I'm talking to about 200 of
you who will be future business leaders,

00:07:33.988 --> 00:07:36.515
I want to talk about what you can do.

00:07:36.515 --> 00:07:41.904
As your companies rethink about your
supply chain in the post-pandemic world,

00:07:41.904 --> 00:07:46.740
it is imperative that you go beyond
the cookie cutter diversification or

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.734
nearshoring strategies that your
consultants keep pitching you about and

00:07:51.734 --> 00:07:54.049
think about the whole ecosystem.

00:07:54.049 --> 00:07:58.803
Resiliency comes from strengthening
the entire supply chain,

00:07:58.803 --> 00:08:02.941
and it begins with investing
in the most fragile links,

00:08:02.941 --> 00:08:06.728
like bringing roads,
bringing electricity, and

00:08:06.728 --> 00:08:11.237
bringing refrigeration to those
villages in Burkina Faso.

00:08:11.237 --> 00:08:15.594
Tudor understood that until
every port had an ice house,

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:18.602
there was no cross Atlantic ice trade.

00:08:18.602 --> 00:08:23.177
Similarly, there is no resilient
global supply chain if farmers

00:08:23.177 --> 00:08:27.850
don't have the infrastructure
to store what they've produced.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:30.445
This is also good for business.

00:08:30.445 --> 00:08:34.984
Better cold storage at the point
of harvest meant fresher,

00:08:34.984 --> 00:08:38.270
higher quality produce for your company.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:42.820
It decreases food loss and also makes for
more predictable supplies.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.728
This will save you money and
is also more sustainable.

00:08:47.728 --> 00:08:53.260
And finally, there's massive social
benefits for the local community.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:59.297
They'll obviously get access to better,
safer, and more nutritious food.

00:08:59.297 --> 00:09:04.142
But more importantly, the same set
of cold storage infrastructure

00:09:04.142 --> 00:09:07.627
will also provide access
to lifesaving drugs and

00:09:07.627 --> 00:09:11.457
vaccines like what we've
seen in the last two years.

00:09:11.457 --> 00:09:15.196
The pandemic gave us
an opportunity to rethink and

00:09:15.196 --> 00:09:19.388
reimagine what the future
cold chain should look like.

00:09:19.388 --> 00:09:23.258
Let's take a page from
the Ice King's playbook and

00:09:23.258 --> 00:09:27.761
start investing in the weakest
link in this global system.

00:09:27.761 --> 00:09:32.781
Only then will we be able to
provide freshness to all,

00:09:32.781 --> 00:09:37.256
resiliently, sustainably, and equitably.

00:09:37.256 --> 00:09:38.468
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:38.468 --> 00:09:43.006
[MUSIC]

